          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you (as a sister).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly avows a loving emotional state. In context, the speaker explicitly qualifies it as sisterly love and cites that affection, alongside admiration and trust, to explain why she would have ventured abroad with him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not itself undertake marriage, travel, service, or another obligation. Indeed, the surrounding speech denies obligation. Her separate conditional willingness to accompany him must not be transferred onto the love avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"The expression is a composed assertion embedded in an explanatory argument, not a reflex-like affective outburst or expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately captured as a qualified avowal of affection under T. Its contextual explanatory role and sisterly qualification do not constitute a separate core mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane addresses St. John during a dispute about whether she is obliged to go to India and whether she must marry him. She explains that her willingness to venture much with him arose from admiration, confidence, and sisterly love, while rejecting both marriage and any claimed obligation to travel.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal love expression makes no undertaking. Separately, Jane says she would accompany St. John as his assistant or sister but not as his wife; he rejects that arrangement as unsuitable and proposes that she assist a married missionary instead. She expressly denies any binding promise or obligation to go to India.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognise the passage as from Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports a represented avowal of sisterly affection, but it cannot independently verify the speaker's inner sincerity beyond her own statement. The precise scope of any earlier informal travel understanding is disputed within the excerpt, though the focal love expression itself is clearly not a promise.\"}"
        }
